In article <1994Jan11.010323.16729@bmers95.bnr.ca>,\
 snelling@bmerh466.bnr.ca (Peter Snelling) writes...
[...]
> CFLAGS = -g -I/usr/include/X11R4 ../include
[...]
> Unfortunately, while it now finds the X11 directory, it no longer finds the
> .../include directory.  So (thinking cleverly) I put it back to:
> CFLAGS = -g -I ../include

     You were missing `-I' in front of `../include'.  I would recommend
using `-I../include -I/usr/include/X11R4' (that is, keeping nethack's
own header set in front of the system ones).  Also, your restored line
should not have a space after the -I.

> My next problem currently has me stumped.  The next error message I got
> when compiling was:
>        cc -g -I../include  -c ../win/X11/dialogs.c
>.../win/X11/dialogs.c: 52: Can't find include file X11/Xaw/Cardinals.h
[...] 
> I looked under the X11 directory, and there was no Xaw subdirectory.
> I couldn't find those files anywhere.  Does anyone know what they are,
> or where I can get them for my machine?

     "Xaw" is the for the Athena Widget set, which is distributed
with MIT's X11 distribution but is not considered part of the core X
implementation.  (If you grabbed the Xaw headers and library, you'd hit
problems with "Xmu" next; those are the miscellaneous utility stuff
that also comes from MIT and used by Xaw.)  Digital does not include
Athena with their X11 (DECwindows) system either, although for Ultrix
it's included in the distribuation as an optionally installed item.
I have no idea whether or not HP provides it that way too.

      You need Xaw and Xmu, but I don't know how you get there from
here.  For Ultrix, I ended up grabbing the entire X11R5 distribution
from the net and then building the client libraries but not the X server.
Using those libraries instead of the DECwindows ones, nethack works
fine, but it was quite a bit of effort for little gain since I generally
only use the tty interface...



